      "latency_ms": 9642.1,
      "prompt_tokens": 25,
      "completion_tokens": 914,
      "output": "\n\nWater is a chemical substance composed of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom, forming the molecule **H₂O**. It is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and colorless liquid at room temperature, though it can exist as a solid (ice) or gas (water vapor) under different conditions. \n\n### Key Characteristics:\n1. **Chemical Structure**: Water's unique properties arise from its **polar molecular structure**. The oxygen atom attracts electrons more strongly than hydrogen, creating a slight negative charge on the oxygen and positive charges on the hydrogens. This polarity enables **hydrogen bonding**, which influences water's high **surface tension**, **specific heat**, and **ability to dissolve many substances**.\n\n2. **Physical Properties**:\n   - **States of Matter**: Liquid (common), solid (ice), and gas (vapor).\n   - **High Specific Heat**: It absorbs and releases heat slowly, helping regulate temperature in organisms and environments.\n   - **Cohesion & Adhesion**: Molecules stick to each other (cohesion) and to other surfaces (adhesion), aiding processes like water transport in plants.\n\n3. **Biological Importance**:\n   - Essential for **life**: All known living organisms require water for biochemical reactions, nutrient transport, and waste removal.\n   - **Solvent Role**: Dissolves ions and molecules, facilitating metabolic processes.\n   - **Human Body**: Makes up ~60% of the human body, crucial for hydration, temperature regulation, and cellular function.\n\n4. **Environmental Role**:\n   - **Water Cycle**: Drives evaporation, condensation, and precipitation, distributing water across Earth.\n   - **Habitat**: Supports aquatic ecosystems and sustains terrestrial life.\n\n5. **Unique Properties**:\n   - **Density Anomaly**: Ice is less dense than liquid water, allowing it to float and insulate aquatic life in cold climates.\n   - **Universal Solvent**: Dissolves more substances than any other liquid, earning it the title \"universal solvent.\"\n\nIn summary, water is a fundamental substance for life, with properties shaped by its molecular structure, making it indispensable for biological, environmental, and physical processes."
